WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.548
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level one.

2
00:00:05.848 --> 00:00:09.524
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.824 --> 00:00:15.774
Unit seventeen: Citizen Journalism. Today you will weigh benefits and risks with 'while' and 'on the other hand'.

4
00:00:16.074 --> 00:00:18.877
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.833 --> 00:00:23.020
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.883 --> 00:00:25.244
Eyewitness account.

7
00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:30.188
Eyewitness account means a description given by a person who saw an event.

8
00:00:30.783 --> 00:00:33.178
An eyewitness account described the storm.

9
00:00:33.783 --> 00:00:36.494
Your turn. Say: Eyewitness account.

10
00:00:39.333 --> 00:00:40.718
Eyewitness account.

11
00:00:41.483 --> 00:00:43.135
Social media post.

12
00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:48.483
Social media post means a message shared on an online platform.

13
00:00:49.083 --> 00:00:51.826
A social media post showed the flood first.

14
00:00:52.417 --> 00:00:55.835
Your turn. Say: Social media post.

15
00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:00.340
Social media post.

16
00:01:01.083 --> 00:01:02.711
User-generated content.

17
00:01:03.717 --> 00:01:07.551
User-generated content means videos or text made by ordinary people.

18
00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:10.881
User-generated content filled the news feed.

19
00:01:11.483 --> 00:01:14.972
Your turn. Say: User-generated content.

20
00:01:17.833 --> 00:01:19.612
User-generated content.

21
00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:22.141
Verification process.

22
00:01:23.150 --> 00:01:27.088
Verification process means the steps used to prove that something is true.

23
00:01:27.683 --> 00:01:29.869
The verification process takes time.

24
00:01:30.483 --> 00:01:33.983
Your turn. Say: Verification process.

25
00:01:36.833 --> 00:01:38.972
Verification process.

26
00:01:39.717 --> 00:01:41.008
First responder.

27
00:01:42.017 --> 00:01:45.816
First responder means a person who arrives first at an emergency.

28
00:01:46.417 --> 00:01:48.521
A first responder helped the injured.

29
00:01:49.117 --> 00:01:52.129
Your turn. Say: First responder.

30
00:01:54.967 --> 00:01:56.304
First responder.

31
00:01:57.050 --> 00:01:58.295
Local reporting.

32
00:01:59.283 --> 00:02:02.548
Local reporting means news gathered by people in the area.

33
00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:06.078
Local reporting reaches places big papers miss.

34
00:02:06.683 --> 00:02:09.684
Your turn. Say: Local reporting.

35
00:02:12.533 --> 00:02:13.929
Local reporting.

36
00:02:14.683 --> 00:02:16.102
Smartphone footage.

37
00:02:17.117 --> 00:02:20.834
Smartphone footage means video recorded on a mobile phone.

38
00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:23.635
Smartphone footage showed the rescue.

39
00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:27.309
Your turn. Say: Smartphone footage.

40
00:02:30.167 --> 00:02:31.655
Smartphone footage.

41
00:02:32.417 --> 00:02:33.917
Community voice.

42
00:02:34.917 --> 00:02:38.774
Community voice means the opinions and experiences of local people.

43
00:02:39.383 --> 00:02:42.776
Citizen journalism gives the community a voice.

44
00:02:43.383 --> 00:02:46.454
Your turn. Say: Community voice.

45
00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:50.545
Community voice.

46
00:02:51.333 --> 00:02:54.856
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:55.717 --> 00:03:01.318
Visal, smartphone footage of the flood spread online before any reporter arrived. Is that good?

48
00:03:02.417 --> 00:03:13.405
In some ways. Citizen journalism is fast and gives the community a voice, while professional news is slower but checked.

49
00:03:14.517 --> 00:03:15.820
What are the risks?

50
00:03:16.917 --> 00:03:25.362
User-generated content can be wrong or old. On the other hand, a verification process can confirm the place and time.

51
00:03:26.450 --> 00:03:27.776
So we need both?

52
00:03:28.883 --> 00:03:37.285
Yes. Eyewitness accounts add detail, and professional reporters add accuracy. Together they give a fuller picture.

53
00:03:38.433 --> 00:03:39.910
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:44.897
Grammar focus: Balancing views: while and on the other hand.

55
00:03:45.197 --> 00:03:46.582
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:47.283 --> 00:03:50.966
Citizen reports are fast, while professional reports are checked.

57
00:03:51.850 --> 00:03:56.151
Footage shows real events. On the other hand, it may be old or edited.

58
00:03:57.050 --> 00:04:00.675
One benefit is speed; one risk is false information.

59
00:04:01.633 --> 00:04:09.708
Use while for contrast inside a sentence and 'on the other hand,' to start a contrasting sentence. Balanced writing gives both benefits and risks.

60
00:04:10.450 --> 00:04:15.796
Be careful! Don't say: Citizen news is fast, but professionals is slow.

61
00:04:17.733 --> 00:04:22.078
Say: Citizen news is fast, while professional news is slow.

62
00:04:22.378 --> 00:04:24.738
Use while to contrast two clauses.

63
00:04:25.533 --> 00:04:28.139
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:28.933 --> 00:04:30.329
Everyone a Reporter?.

65
00:04:30.629 --> 00:04:33.581
A smartphone can turn any witness into a reporter.

66
00:04:34.567 --> 00:04:39.190
An eyewitness account on a social media post may reach thousands within minutes.

67
00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:44.684
First responders and neighbours provide local reporting that big newsrooms may miss.

68
00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:52.924
On the other hand, user-generated content needs a careful verification process, because pictures can be old or edited.

69
00:04:53.933 --> 00:04:57.976
Citizen journalism is most valuable when it works with trained editors.

70
00:04:59.067 --> 00:05:03.022
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:03.883 --> 00:05:07.601
Citizen reports are fast, blank professional reports are checked.

72
00:05:07.901 --> 00:05:11.647
A, while. B, because. C, so that.

73
00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:16.909
The answer is A: while.

74
00:05:18.050 --> 00:05:21.373
A description given by someone who saw an event is an blank.

75
00:05:21.673 --> 00:05:25.942
A, exposé. B, update. C, eyewitness account.

76
00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:31.755
The answer is C: eyewitness account.

77
00:05:32.917 --> 00:05:35.938
Videos made by ordinary people are blank content.

78
00:05:36.238 --> 00:05:40.007
A, user-generated. B, rolling. C, primary.

79
00:05:43.567 --> 00:05:45.949
The answer is A: user-generated.

80
00:05:47.083 --> 00:05:49.245
Choose the best balancing phrase.

81
00:05:49.545 --> 00:05:56.635
A, On the other hand, it may be false.. B, In the other hand, it may false.. C, On other hand it is false..

82
00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:04.173
The answer is A: On the other hand, it may be false..

83
00:06:05.317 --> 00:06:08.593
According to the reading, citizen journalism works best with…

84
00:06:08.893 --> 00:06:13.011
A, trained editors. B, no checks. C, rumours.

85
00:06:16.567 --> 00:06:18.926
The answer is A: trained editors.

86
00:06:20.133 --> 00:06:23.355
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:24.217 --> 00:06:29.560
What are the benefits and risks of relying on citizen journalism during a fast-moving event?

88
00:06:38.350 --> 00:06:39.920
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:40.220 --> 00:07:02.719
One benefit is speed, because witnesses can share footage within seconds, and they show the community's voice. On the other hand, there is a serious risk of false or edited content. Strangers may also spread rumours without checking. Therefore, I would trust citizen reports only after a verification process.

90
00:07:03.019 --> 00:07:04.798
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:05.683 --> 00:07:11.261
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and balancing views: while and on the other hand.

92
00:07:11.561 --> 00:07:16.095
Today's value is critical reasoning. Test every argument before you accept it.

93
00:07:16.395 --> 00:07:19.035
See you in Unit eighteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:19.335 --> 00:07:22.417
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

